@@ -42,6 +42,24 @@ app.use(async (event) => {
 >
 > The examples below use a hardcoded value so they stay readable, but in a real app load a randomly generated secret of at least 32 characters from an environment variable such as `process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD`, and never commit it to source control. A guessable passphrase (even one ≥32 characters) is not safe.
 
+### Rotating the password
+
+Changing `password` signs out every user whose cookie was sealed with the old one. To rotate it without that, move the old password to `extraUnsealPasswords`:
+
+```js
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD, // new password, used to seal
+  extraUnsealPasswords: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD], // still opens existing sessions
+});
+```
+
+- Only `password` seals. Each cookie opened with an extra password is resealed with `password` on the same response, so the rotation completes as users make requests.
+- Passwords are tried in order (`password`, then each extra), one PBKDF2 open per password. Each extra adds CPU cost to requests with cookies that do not open with the earlier passwords, and `legacySealFallback` doubles it. Keep the list short.
+- Remove the old password once `maxAge` or `idleTimeout` has passed since the last cookie was sealed with it. Without either option, old sessions never expire, so remove it only after users have signed in again.
+- Sessions sent in the `x-{name}-session` header are accepted with an extra password but not resealed, so they keep the old password until they expire.
+- Every extra password must meet the same rules as `password` (at least 32 characters). A short extra placed before the password that sealed a cookie makes that cookie unreadable.
+- Keep old passwords in environment variables, not in source control.
+
 This will initialize a session and return an header `Set-Cookie` with a cookie named `h3` and an encrypted content.
 
 If the request contains a cookie named `h3` or a header named `x-h3-session`, the session will be initialized with the content of the cookie or the header.
